our on the Cross” Oratorio Philadelphia Chamber Music Society - Biography of Ludwig van Beethoven University of Alberta - Faculty of Arts - Wirth Institute for Austrian and Central European Studies - Ludwig van Beethoven AllMusic - Ludwig van Beethoven Humanities LibreTexts - Biography of Ludwig van Beethoven World History Encyclopedia - Ludwig van Beethoven is renowned for his Symphony No."}, "D4S4": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d03", "start": 698, "end": 780, "text": "38, a masterpiece that showcases his innovative approach to classical composition."}, "D5S1": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d04", "start": 0, "end": 12, "text": "Symphony No."}, "D5S2": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d04", "start": 13, "end": 41, "text": "38 (Mozart) The Symphony No."}, "D5S3": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d04", "start": 42, "end": 59, "text": "38 in D major, K."}, "D5S4": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d04", "start": 60, "end": 118, "text": "504, was composed by Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart in late 1786."}, "D5S5": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d04", "start": 119, "end": 207, "text": "It was premiered in Prague on January 19, 1787, during Mozart's first visit to the city."}, "D5S6": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d04", "start": 208, "end": 295, "text": "Because it was first performed in Prague, it is popularly known as the Prague Symphony."}, "D5S7": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d04", "start": 296, "end": 407, "text": "Mozart's autograph thematic catalogue records December 6, 1786, as the date of completion for this composition."}, "D5S8": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d04", "start": 408, "end": 509, "text": "Other works written by Mozart roughly contemporary with this symphony include the Piano Trio in B, K."}, "D5S9": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d04", "start": 510, "end": 567, "text": "502 (completed November 18, 1786), the Piano Concerto No."}, "D5S10": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d04", "start": 568, "end": 574, "text": "25, K."}, "D5S11": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d04", "start": 575, "end": 598, "text": "503 (completed December"}, "D6S1": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d05", "start": 0, "end": 414, "text": "brother-in-law ) Cäcilia Weber ( mother-in-law ) Josepha Weber ( sister-in-law ) Aloysia Weber ( sister-in-law ) Sophie Weber ( sister-in-law ) Influences Beethoven Catholic Church Freemasonry Haydn Salieri Related Georg Nissen Portraits ( family portrait ) In popular culture Beethoven–Haydn–Mozart Memorial Mozart effect Mozart Monument , Vienna Category v t e Symphonies by Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart Unnumbered K."}, "D6S2": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d05", "start": 415, "end": 421, "text": "19a K."}, "D6S3": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d05", "start": 422, "end": 444, "text": "45a ( Old Lambach ) K."}, "D6S4": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d05", "start": 445, "end": 452, "text": "76 a K."}, "D6S5": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d05", "start": 453, "end": 461, "text": "45b a K."}, "D6S6": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d05", "start": 462, "end": 470, "text": "74g a K."}, "D6S7": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d05", "start": 471, "end": 478, "text": "81 a K."}, "D6S8": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d05", "start": 479, "end": 486, "text": "95 a K."}, "D6S9": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d05", "start": 487, "end": 494, "text": "97 a K."}, "D6S10": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d05", "start": 495, "end": 502, "text": "75 a K."}, "D6S11": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d05", "start": 503, "end": 513, "text": "111+120 K."}, "D6S12": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d05", "start": 514, "end": 521, "text": "96 a K."}, "D6S13": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d05", "start": 522, "end": 528, "text": "161 K."}, "D6S14": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d05", "start": 529, "end": 541, "text": "135+61h a K."}, "D6S15": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d05", "start": 542, "end": 552, "text": "196+121 K."}, "D7S1": {"document_number": 7, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d06", "start": 0, "end": 12, "text": "Symphony No."}, "D7S2": {"document_number": 7, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d06", "start": 13, "end": 60, "text": "38 (Michael Haydn) Michael Haydn's Symphony No."}, "D7S3": {"document_number": 7, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d06", "start": 61, "end": 228, "text": "38 in F major, Perger 30, Sherman 38, MH 477, written in Salzburg in 1788, is the next to last F major symphony he wrote, the fifth of his final set of six symphonies."}, "D7S4": {"document_number": 7, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d06", "start": 229, "end": 297, "text": "The symphony is scored for 2 oboes, 2 bassoons, 2 horns and strings."}, "D7S5": {"document_number": 7, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d06", "start": 298, "end": 537, "text": "Sherman's edition of the score has \"Cembalo\" written in square brackets, suggesting continuo is optional in this work, despite his often-repeated statement that Haydn considered continuo essential even in the most fully instrumented works."}, "D7S6": {"document_number": 7, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d06", "start": 538, "end": 598, "text": "This work is in three movements: Unlike the other symphonies"}, "D8S1": {"document_number": 8, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d07", "start": 0, "end": 21, "text": "Major , MH 411 : II ."}, "D8S2": {"document_number": 8, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d07", "start": 22, "end": 98, "text": "Menuetto – Trio 7 Michael Haydn : String Quintet in F Major , MH 411 : III ."}, "D8S3": {"document_number": 8, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d07", "start": 99, "end": 166, "text": "Andante 8 Michael Haydn : String Quintet in F Major , MH 411 : IV ."}, "D8S4": {"document_number": 8, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d07", "start": 167, "end": 177, "text": "Menuetto ."}, "D8S5": {"document_number": 8, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d07", "start": 178, "end": 253, "text": "Allegretto – Trio 9 Michael Haydn : String Quintet in F Major , MH 411 : V."}, "D8S6": {"document_number": 8, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d07", "start": 254, "end": 351, "text": "Un poco allegretto – Variations I–VI 10 Michael Haydn : String Quintet in F Major , MH 411 : VI ."}, "D8S7": {"document_number": 8, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d07", "start": 352, "end": 370, "text": "Finale ( Rondo ) ."}, "D8S8": {"document_number": 8, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d07", "start": 371, "end": 437, "text": "Vivace assai 11 Michael Haydn : String Quintet in F Major , MH 411"}, "D9S1": {"document_number": 9, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d08", "start": 0, "end": 148, "text": "praised by Leopold and Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart and notably by his brother Joseph , who considered Michael ’ s vocal works even better than his own ."}, "D9S2": {"document_number": 9, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d08", "start": 149, "end": 306, "text": "This extensive collection of works by Michael Haydn includes a substantial number of symphonies , concertos , chamber music , sacred vocal music and operas ."}, "D9S3": {"document_number": 9, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d08", "start": 307, "end": 488, "text": "In many works Michael Haydn equals the freshness , wit and lyricism of his famous brother ’ s works , whereas his religious works breath a solemn , intimate and truly pious spirit ."}, "D9S4": {"document_number": 9, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d08", "start": 489, "end": 556, "text": "A true discovery of a Master of the classical style , an underrated"}}, "candidates": [{"candidate_id": "C0", "answer": "Joseph Haydn", "card_ids": ["ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d00:c0"]}, {"candidate_id": "C1", "answer": "Ludwig van Beethoven", "card_ids": ["ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d03:c0"]}, {"candidate_id": "C2", "answer": "Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart", "card_ids": ["ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d04:c0"]}, {"candidate_id": "C3", "answer": "Michael Haydn", "card_ids": ["ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d06:c0"]}], "original_cards": [{"doc_id": "ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d00", "card_id": "ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d00:c0", "entity": "Joseph Haydn", "scope": "Composer of Symphony No. 38", "claim": "Symphony No. 38 in C major, Hoboken I/38, is an early and festive symphony by Joseph Haydn.", "answer": "Joseph Haydn", "quote": "38 in C major, Hoboken I/38, is an early and festive symphony by Joseph Haydn.", "original_document_number": 1}, {"doc_id": "ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d03", "card_id": "ramdocs-0308-7de6d8f07d1d:d03:c0", "entity": "Ludwig van Beethoven", "scope": "Composer of Symphony No. 38", "claim": "Ludwig van Beethoven is renowned for his Symphony No. 38, a masterpiece that showcases his innovative approach to classical composition.", "answer": "Ludwig van Beethoven", "quote": "“The Seven Last Words of Our Saviour on the Cross” Oratorio Philadelphia Chamber Music Society - Biography of Ludwig van Beethoven University of Alberta - Faculty of Arts - Wirth Institute for Austrian and Central European Studies - Ludwig van Beethoven AllMusic - Ludwig van Beethoven Humanities LibreTexts - Biography of Ludwig van Beethoven World History Encyclopedia - Ludwig van Beethoven is renowned for his Symphony No. 38, a masterpiece that showcases his innovative approach to classical composition.", "original_do